      "seed": 1391275182,
      "version": 69,
      "versionNonce": 1568189938,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764395698589,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🟩 5. How JSP → Servlet Conversion Works (Actual Example)\n\nSuppose your JSP:\n\n<h1>Hello <%= name %></h1>\n\n\nTomcat will convert it into something like:\n\npublic class hello_jsp extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void _jspService(HttpServletRequest req,\nHttpServletResponse resp) {\n        out.write(\"<h1>Hello \");\n        out.write(name);\n        out.write(\"</h1>\");\n    }\n}\n\n✔ Every JSP becomes a Servlet\n✔ JSP is just a shortcut for writing HTML-generating Servlets",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🟩 5. How JSP → Servlet Conversion Works (Actual Example)\n\nSuppose your JSP:\n\n<h1>Hello <%= name %></h1>\n\n\nTomcat will convert it into something like:\n\npublic class hello_jsp extends HttpServlet {\n    protected void _jspService(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp) {\n        out.write(\"<h1>Hello \");\n        out.write(name);\n        out.write(\"</h1>\");\n    }\n}\n\n✔ Every JSP becomes a Servlet\n✔ JSP is just a shortcut for writing HTML-generating Servlets",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
