      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764398922569,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "❓ If a Servlet handles request/response,\nthen why do we convert JSP (UI) into a\nServlet?",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "❓ If a Servlet handles request/response, then why do we convert JSP (UI) into a Servlet?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FfDgtrAgZHwe6aptHCqz-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 175.16854107399803,
      "y": 34169.88515981215,
      "width": 654.4609375,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "baF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1057908334,
      "version": 71,
      "versionNonce": 781300210,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764398942506,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Because of ONE SIMPLE REASON:\n\n⭐ Tomcat can ONLY execute Java code. JSP is not Java code.\n\nLet’s break it down properly 👇\n\n🟦 1. Servlet = Java code (logic)\n\nServlets are pure Java classes:\n\nThey can run inside Tomcat\n\nThey can be executed\n\nThey can handle HTTP requests\n\nThey can write responses (HTML, JSON, text)\n\nBut…\n\n❌ SERVLETS ARE VERY BAD FOR WRITING HTML\n\nExample of servlet returning HTML:\n\nout.println(\"<html>\");\nout.println(\"<body>\");\nout.println(\"<h1>Hello User</h1>\");\nout.println(\"</body></html>\");\n\n\nThis is ugly and painful.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Because of ONE SIMPLE REASON:\n\n⭐ Tomcat can ONLY execute Java code. JSP is not Java code.\n\nLet’s break it down properly 👇\n\n🟦 1. Servlet = Java code (logic)\n\nServlets are pure Java classes:\n\nThey can run inside Tomcat\n\nThey can be executed\n\nThey can handle HTTP requests\n\nThey can write responses (HTML, JSON, text)\n\nBut…\n\n❌ SERVLETS ARE VERY BAD FOR WRITING HTML\n\nExample of servlet returning HTML:\n\nout.println(\"<html>\");\nout.println(\"<body>\");\nout.println(\"<h1>Hello User</h1>\");\nout.println(\"</body></html>\");\n\n\nThis is ugly and painful.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2WDluwpd6pd4zLC1pXW_j",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 173.02115964363145,
      "y": 34760.74011997142,
      "width": 513.2564212015233,
      "height": 120.86741663905559,
