      "seed": 1936811890,
      "version": 34,
      "versionNonce": 372966126,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1764404450627,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🟧 6. Why Spring MVC Does NOT Replace Session\n\nBecause:\n\n👍 Model = Temporary data\n👍 Session = Persistent data per user\n👉 They serve DIFFERENT purposes\n\nSo Spring gives you:\n\n✔ Model → request scope\n✔ @SessionAttributes / HttpSession → session scope\n\nIf you want Spring to store something in session, you do:\n\nsession.setAttribute(\"user\", userObject);\n\n\nor Spring-style:\n\n@GetMapping(\"/login\")\npublic String login(HttpSession session) {\n    session.setAttribute(\"user\", \"Shiv\");\n    return \"dashboard.jsp\";\n}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🟧 6. Why Spring MVC Does NOT Replace Session\n\nBecause:\n\n👍 Model = Temporary data\n👍 Session = Persistent data per user\n👉 They serve DIFFERENT purposes\n\nSo Spring gives you:\n\n✔ Model → request scope\n✔ @SessionAttributes / HttpSession → session scope\n\nIf you want Spring to store something in session, you do:\n\nsession.setAttribute(\"user\", userObject);\n\n\nor Spring-style:\n\n@GetMapping(\"/login\")\npublic String login(HttpSession session) {\n    session.setAttribute(\"user\", \"Shiv\");\n    return \"dashboard.jsp\";\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
